  "query_preview": "..High pressure will settle over the Four Corners region \ntoday, then pushes slightly to the east this week. This high will be \nin a very favorable position for increases monsoon activity as \nmoisture advects into the region on the strengthening southeasterly \nflow. \n\nShowers and isolated thunderstorms continue early this morning \nacross the region as remnant outflows gradually move through the \narea. While these overnight storms are not expected to be severe, \nsome brief heavy rain and strong, gusty winds will be possible as \nthe move off to the west/northwest through daybreak. \n\nFor today, moisture will continue to increase across the region and \nwe will see scattered to widespread showers develop. With the high \nin place over the Four Corners and the moisture surging into the \nregion, we will see cooler temperatures across the area, and no heat \nheadlines are expected for at least the next several day. Storms are \nexpected to initiate across the higher terrain by early afternoon, \nwith a west to northwest motion. The increase in moisture will \nresult in PWATs between 1.00-1.50\", and storms will be capable of \nproducing heavy/very heavy rainfall with flash flooding becoming a \nprimary concern. With the activity early this morning, we may have \nmore debris cloud coverage which in turn may delay storm initiation \ninto this afternoon. \n\nThe active monsoon looks to continue through much of the week with \ndaily scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms. Heavy rain \nleading to flash flooding will be the main hazard of concern through \nthe week. Several models are pointing to a disturbance pushing up \nfrom Sonora which may help to keep convection going during eh \novernight periods. By Friday, some dry air will start to push into \nthe region. However, we do not fully dry out and while it may not be \nas active there will still be showers and thunderstorms around.",
